#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0746683d-8797-5f2b-95dc-71f40b0506c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545215} {:answer/id #uuid "8418df2d-a794-5d2d-8b95-e9c9f6f2cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545449} {:answer/id #uuid "c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545559} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cd1ffe-fc48-5e5c-b643-f8b5e62deab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545562} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a98074-8d83-5bec-84ca-c92ae7444892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7711a92-f975-5159-8094-830ed6cf3bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545559} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e4fe6a-97c0-55f8-aa37-67b07ae4b10b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "407bfc4b-29e1-5ad8-862e-1c4f1b286f41" :question/math-academy-id "q-117549" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccdbd48b6cdf40205fe15a0b52553926aa4ba5e66e5a29bcf9ce6ae4164154a4.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠v.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50d1e010a8279bf8f59a5e57b4ee4d4b744af7e431351bb17397e123aa79c59a.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${100}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${36}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={36}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ce/ce9944a3fa28177bd0882fccd53d544159b6f041c570e7037cac7db31103130e.png)\n\nThe angles $∠v$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combined give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠v+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{100}^{∘}+{36}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v+{136}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠v & ={44}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479d9a01-9deb-5a09-aa6b-934264ad1bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545335} {:answer/id #uuid "68b20937-06fa-5ee7-9f09-8c2eff9900a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545398} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa510db-507e-5d1c-ac87-823b20e24290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545401} {:answer/id #uuid "a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545502} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa1e72-858f-5fb1-b6b1-a45409e1345a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5702a25-959c-54e4-8b48-d733d466e381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545502} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce27563-7842-5bd7-8582-3e404e021713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d28d4f3-c7e7-5aa9-96b5-112441d6c1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-117567" :question/problem "![](images/47/4745f9e651eb50c345550e83ee82eb957cd28fd82e3398b667e4285bb512194c.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{AB}∥\\overset{⟷}{CD}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $∠JID$ and $∠EJB$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠JID & =m∠EJB \\\\ 4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-x \\\\ 5x+{25}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={155}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{{155}^{∘}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & ={31}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={31}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bb692e-36e7-518e-934f-7f2348cb810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545229} {:answer/id #uuid "4552359d-6331-5661-8b66-027569a2f7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545331} {:answer/id #uuid "4903221c-fcab-5a64-8b23-188c2c939e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545337} {:answer/id #uuid "df394d85-dc28-5c29-8329-dc16aae0f557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545611} {:answer/id #uuid "facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "facc42be-49b3-5141-9e80-3358d50482bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545664} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d96c227-6f23-514a-8097-fbbd94755da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90e45430-ff4c-59cd-b8f8-806fae001939" :question/math-academy-id "q-53171" :question/problem "![](images/db/db1b39e3f00fd6bb64ed738fb12213f4775d2bd7d098dfa9d0efec2192d7a64f.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$" :question/worked-solution "Consider $∠2$ and $∠3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a775b7c9ce63aa5b9f8c03fb91e7018d2f1edde5a2a2acdb099ae5ba53a874e4.png)\n\nNote that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${75}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠2={75}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $∠3$ and the angle whose measure is ${50}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore, \n\n$$\nm∠3={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/01/01462502a4fbe0b25e489cc611200beb47cd926b8a055b69b7279aa0f301850b.png)\n\nThe angles $∠1$, $∠2,$ and $∠3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2+m∠3 & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{75}^{∘}+{50}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{125}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={55}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
